/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  chemistry/H2Glut-formation.dat -- the glutaric acid master as the sum of its fully dissociated form (the inverse of the records written from the master; a case declares one master and the closure activates the record reachable from it):
      Glut(-2) + 2 H+  <=>  H2Glut
  Made by bin/curate/species_c373.py from data/curation/batches/species_c373/*.tsv.

  logK25 and dH are the PRINTED values of the sources below (standard state ideal solution at 1 mol/kg, 298.15 K):
      pK1 = 4.34, dH1 = -0.544 kJ/mol
      pK2 = 5.42, dH2 = -2.385 kJ/mol
  Source: Shock, Organic acids in hydrothermal solutions: standard molal thermodynamic properties of carboxylic acids, and estimates of dissociation constants at high temperatures and pressures, Am. J. Sci. 295 (1995) 496-580; H+ = 0, 25 C, 1 bar, hypothetical 1 molal ideal solution (Appendix 1); cal/mol and cal/mol/K converted at 4.184 exactly; a superscript on a table value names how the author obtained it (Table 4 footnotes, p. 519; Table 10 footnotes, p. 544).
  This record carries the sum of the first 2 steps (the sign flips with the direction written).
  The constant-dCp term of the printed dCp is NOT carried: the record grammar has no place for it (a
  van't Hoff integral with a constant dCp is exactly the `analytic` A1, A3, A4 terms; proposed, not done).
  The dH is the difference of formation enthalpies at least one of which is not a measurement; the
  `provenance { dH {} }` block below carries the word of the least certain one, and the engine announces
  it whenever a run moves log K with this dH (a temperature other than 298.15 K).
  The neutral master carries its own aqueous formation data (no component + solution pair is in the catalogue for this acid).  Source: Shock (1995); values the paper marks as estimated carry `origin estimated` per value.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species H2Glut; masters ( { ion Glut; nu 1; } { ion H; nu 2; } );
      logK25 9.76; dH 2929; source "Shock, Organic acids in hydrothermal solutions: standard molal thermodynamic properties of carboxylic acids, and estimates of dissociation constants at high temperatures and pressures, Am. J. Sci. 295 (1995) 496-580 -- pK: Table 7, p. 534 (log K at 25 C, as printed), dH: the printed DfH of the anion minus that of the acid in Table 4, p. 518 (PDF p. 23) / Table 4, p. 519 (PDF p. 24): pK1 4.34, dH1 -0.544 kJ/mol (read from a page image), pK2 5.42, dH2 -2.385 kJ/mol (read from a page image) (pK, dH_ion at 298.15 K)";

provenance
{
    dH
    {
        origin  estimated;
        method  "the difference of the formation enthalpies hfAq of H2Glut (literature), HGlut (estimated), Glut (estimated) in their species records, so it carries the word of its least certain input (HGlut, Glut: Shock (1995) Table 4 footnote ab: DfH computed from the tabulated DfG and S (S estimated, footnotes af and ae) with the elements' entropies)";
    }
}
